  <h1>India’s Defence Indigenization Push — Atmanirbhar Bharat in Security</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>In 2024, India accelerated its push for defence indigenization, aligning with the broader Atmanirbhar Bharat vision. The Hindu emphasized how reducing dependence on imports, promoting domestic manufacturing, and encouraging private participation are critical to ensuring national security and economic growth. Defence indigenization is not only about self-reliance but also about strategic autonomy in a volatile global order.
________________________________________
Current Defence Landscape
•	Import Dependence: India is among the world’s top three arms importers (SIPRI, 2023).
•	Budget Allocation: Defence budget 2024 ~₹6.2 lakh crore, with ~25% earmarked for capital procurement.
•	Domestic Progress: Indigenous production worth ~₹1 lakh crore achieved in 2023–24.
•	Exports: Defence exports crossed ₹21,000 crore, highest-ever, with buyers in 80+ countries.
________________________________________
Key Policy Initiatives
1.	Negative Import Lists
o	MoD announced successive lists banning import of 500+ items.
o	Encouraged local industry to fill gaps.
2.	Defence Production & PLI
o	Incentives for domestic manufacturing of drones, small arms, and electronics.
3.	Private Sector & Startups
o	iDEX (Innovations for Defence Excellence) program funding defence startups.
o	Tata, L&T, Bharat Forge emerging alongside DPSUs.
4.	Strategic Projects
o	Indigenous aircraft carrier INS Vikrant (2022).
o	Light Combat Aircraft (LCA) Tejas induction.
o	BrahMos missile exports to Philippines.
________________________________________
Challenges
•	Technology Gaps: Still dependent on imports for jet engines, stealth tech, and high-end electronics.
•	R&D Weakness: Defence R&D investment <1% of GDP.
•	Bureaucratic Delays: Procurement and licensing remain complex.
•	Skill Deficits: Need for advanced manufacturing expertise.
•	Export Barriers: Competing with established global players.
________________________________________
Strategic Significance
•	National Security: Reduces vulnerability during conflicts and sanctions.
•	Economic Growth: Boosts manufacturing, jobs, and innovation.
•	Global Standing: Defence exports improve India’s status as net security provider in Indo-Pacific.
•	Diplomacy: Arms exports strengthen strategic partnerships.
________________________________________
UPSC Relevance
•	GS-III (Security): Defence reforms, indigenization, self-reliance.
•	GS-II (IR): Strategic autonomy, defence diplomacy.
•	Essay:
o	“Self-reliance in defence is the foundation of national security.”
o	“Technology and security: the twin pillars of sovereignty.”
•	PSIR Paper II: Military-industrial complex; strategic autonomy in IR.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Boost R&D: Expand DRDO–private collaboration; increase defence R&D funding.<br/>2.	Global Partnerships: Co-develop technology with allies (US, France, Israel).<br/>3.	Skill Development: Specialized training for advanced defence manufacturing.<br/>4.	Ease of Business: Simplify licensing, procurement, and export procedures.<br/>5.	Focus on Niche Areas: Drones, AI in warfare, cyber defence, space militarization.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s defence indigenization drive is a strategic imperative, not just an economic program. Progress in naval assets, missiles, and aircraft is encouraging, but success hinges on addressing technology gaps and building strong industry–academia linkages. For UPSC aspirants, this issue illustrates how security, economy, and governance converge in shaping India’s rise.</p>
